latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Scan the entire grid.\n   b. For each non-black color, record:\n      - The leftmost and rightmost column it appears in.\n      - All cell coordinates where it appears.\n\n2. Sort the colors:\n   - Sort the colors based on their leftmost appearance in the input grid.\n\n3. Process each color:\n   a. Determine the width of the color's presence (rightmost - leftmost + 1).\n   b. Sort the color's cells by column (left to right), then by row (bottom to top).\n   c. Create a new consolidated block for this color:\n      - Start filling from the bottom-left of the block.\n      - Maintain the width determined in step 3a.\n      - Fill upwards until all cells of this color are placed.\n\n4. Place processed color blocks in the new grid:\n   a. Start from the leftmost column of the new grid.\n   b. For each processed color block:\n      - Place it at the bottom of the grid.\n      - Align its left edge with the current column.\n      - Move the current column to the right by the width of this color block.\n\n5. Final consolidation:\n   - Scan each column of the new grid from bottom to top.\n   - If there are any gaps (black cells) between colored cells in a column, \n     move all colored cells down to eliminate these gaps.\n\n6. Create and return the new ColoredGrid object with the transformed grid.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects we identified in our reflection:\n- It preserves colors and their left-to-right order (steps 1, 2, and 4).\n- It consolidates vertically while maintaining overall width (step 3).\n- It ensures bottom alignment and creates a skyline effect (steps 4 and 5).\n- It handles both connected and disconnected color regions (step 3).\n\nBy following this plan, we should be able to transform the input grid according to the patterns observed in all test cases."
